1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
They're fast.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They're cheap.

3
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
They're kitchens on wheels.

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
It's fresh, warm food to the top side.

5
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:18,000
They can be as small as a pushcart or as big as an 18-wheel tractor trailer.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Food trucks are multiplying all across America.

7
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:29,000
But their roots go back more than a century, to the days of the cowboys and the wildfires.

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:30,740
of cowboys in the Wild West.

9
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:35,380
Many of today's food trucks are triumphs of precision engineering.

10
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:42,080
From a massive pig sheathed in fiberglass.

11
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:44,480
Never thought I'd see myself ordering from a giant pig.

12
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:48,780
To a double-decker pizza kitchen with a real wood-burning oven.

13
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,460
Food trucks are definitely a modern marvel.

14
00:00:52,461 --> 00:00:55,860
Now, food trucks on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:10,500
In the heart of our nation's capital,

16
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:15,440
dozens of food trucks gather for a 12-hour food truck festival.

17
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:19,280
The place? An out-of-the-way parking lot.

18
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:21,660
But you wouldn't know it for the crowds.

19
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,780
More than 20,000 people will come here today for one thing only.

20
00:01:26,781 --> 00:01:28,160
The food trucks.

21
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:32,360
two dozen varieties of foods brought to them on wheels.

22
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,500
It took a long time to get this, but it was definitely working.

23
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:39,740
And this gathering is not unique.

24
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:44,880
In cities and towns across America, food trucks are becoming a fixture.

25
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:48,760
Yet there's a lot more to these colorful trucks than meets the eye.

26
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,920
Years of technological innovation have transformed them

27
00:01:52,921 --> 00:01:56,800
from simple carts to ingenious kitchens on wheels,

28
00:01:56,801 --> 00:02:02,880
Filling a need for workers who don't have time for a sit-down restaurant.

29
00:02:02,881 --> 00:02:05,600
And tempting others with an array of unusual foods.

30
00:02:05,601 --> 00:02:09,480
It tastes seriously like the cake donut.

31
00:02:09,481 --> 00:02:13,360
But as you might imagine, feeding crowds like this from a kitchen not much bigger than a

32
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:18,620
pool table calls for high-tech engineering and teamwork.

33
00:02:18,621 --> 00:02:23,440
Even small restaurants often have kitchens over a thousand square feet.

34
00:02:23,441 --> 00:02:27,940
The average food truck's kitchen is about 7 feet wide by 16 feet long.

35
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:30,620
That's only about 100 square feet of kitchen space.

36
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,460
And that's not all these food trucks have to juggle.

37
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:39,540
They're off the grid, which means they must carry a generator for electricity,

38
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:42,700
propane for their griddles and fryers,

39
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:45,200
and water for cooking and cleaning.

40
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:48,680
Not to mention all their supplies for a day on the road.

41
00:02:48,681 --> 00:02:55,680
Making a truck that can handle all these demands is a very specialized business.

42
00:02:55,681 --> 00:03:03,680
Many entrepreneurs go directly to companies like Arminco Catering Truck in Los Angeles, California.

43
00:03:03,681 --> 00:03:08,680
You thinking solid doors or do you want to leave them open? Solid doors?

44
00:03:08,681 --> 00:03:15,680
In a 13,000 square foot warehouse, Arminco makes food trucks one at a time.

45
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:23,680
We don't have robots working here, so it's all done by hand by skilled craftsmen.

46
00:03:23,681 --> 00:03:27,680
And each unit, like fingerprints, every single unit is different

47
00:03:27,681 --> 00:03:30,680
because each client has different needs, each state has different requirements,

48
00:03:30,681 --> 00:03:35,680
and we pride ourselves on improving each and every truck that we build.

49
00:03:35,681 --> 00:03:41,680
Arthur and his team of 16 men start with basic trucks, new and used.

50
00:03:41,681 --> 00:03:46,680
Then they retrofit them into food trucks by remodeling their structures,

51
00:03:46,681 --> 00:03:51,480
carving into the 1 1⁄8-inch aluminum walls with plasma cutters and saws,

52
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:54,700
and installing custom kitchens inside.

53
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:58,220
It's a process that takes about five to six months

54
00:03:58,221 --> 00:04:02,880
to turn an ordinary truck into an extraordinary food truck.

55
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:07,260
Their most popular order begins with a basic used delivery truck,

56
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:13,280
18 to 22 feet long and sturdy enough to handle the additional weight of a kitchen.

57
00:04:13,281 --> 00:04:21,000
Loaded up with equipment, water and fuel, a typical 18 to 22 foot food truck can weigh

58
00:04:21,001 --> 00:04:24,400
up to 15,000 pounds.

59
00:04:24,401 --> 00:04:29,620
So they add overload springs to increase weight carrying capability.

60
00:04:29,621 --> 00:04:34,500
But first, the Armenco team addresses the delivery truck's main drawback, its dropped

61
00:04:34,501 --> 00:04:36,080
floor.

62
00:04:36,081 --> 00:04:41,600
This was an important feature in the truck's former life for easy loading and unloading.

63
00:04:41,601 --> 00:04:46,080
The Armenco team levels the floor and extends the chassis with steel crossmembers.

64
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:50,900
Because of the drop floor, this did not have a chassis at this rearmost section.

65
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:54,540
That's not going to work for a kitchen. We need a contiguous floor at level.

66
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:59,260
Next, the team cuts out top vents and skylights to bring in fresh air

67
00:04:59,261 --> 00:05:04,040
and inserts holes for specialty items, like speakers, to lure crowds.

68
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:07,120
One day somebody might be drinking a soda out of this.

69
00:05:07,121 --> 00:05:12,120
But every time they cut away part of the structure, it weakens the rest.

70
00:05:12,121 --> 00:05:19,120
So each section is framed or thickened with 2-inch aluminum tubing to accommodate that weakness.

71
00:05:19,121 --> 00:05:25,120
Once the framing is complete, the team installs a 30-gallon propane tank to power the cooking appliances.

72
00:05:25,121 --> 00:05:31,120
Plus, water and wastewater tanks and a generator for power.

73
00:05:31,121 --> 00:05:36,120
So with all that propane and diesel fuel, is the truck a rolling time bomb?

74
00:05:36,121 --> 00:05:39,120
The fact of the matter is no, it's actually quite safe.

75
00:05:39,121 --> 00:05:43,120
When you want to cause a propane tank to explode,

76
00:05:43,121 --> 00:05:46,120
it takes a whole heck of a lot of work.

77
00:05:46,121 --> 00:05:51,120
But just in case, state regulations call for fire suppression systems

78
00:05:51,121 --> 00:05:56,120
that will shut down appliances, as well as emergency fire exits.

79
00:05:56,121 --> 00:06:00,120
Now comes the tricky part, how to fit an entire kitchen into a space

80
00:06:00,121 --> 00:06:03,120
100 square feet in size.

81
00:06:03,121 --> 00:06:07,880
Food truck kitchens come in all shapes and sizes, with special equipment and different

82
00:06:07,881 --> 00:06:12,100
layouts designed to fit the client's needs.

83
00:06:12,101 --> 00:06:16,780
But a common food truck kitchen layout is horseshoe shape for easy access.

84
00:06:16,781 --> 00:06:23,100
A griddle sits in the center, framed by a fryer, a warmer oven, and a prep table.

85
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:28,660
Every cubic foot is utilized, and every appliance custom made.

86
00:06:28,661 --> 00:06:31,680
The refrigerator is designed to contain spillage.

87
00:06:31,681 --> 00:06:37,500
built with a lockdown door and deep sides. The deep fat fryer also has a

88
00:06:37,501 --> 00:06:43,980
lockdown lid which closes itself when the truck is in motion. Even the sinks are

89
00:06:43,981 --> 00:06:48,920
special guaranteed not to leak water into the walls.

90
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:54,260
Finally the floors are plated with stainless steel which is curved at the

91
00:06:54,261 --> 00:06:59,860
edges of the appliances to keep the whole kitchen watertight. For the

92
00:06:59,861 --> 00:07:03,640
Finishing touches, the team wraps the truck with custom-designed graphics.

93
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:07,040
It's pretty much like a big paint job, the only difference is just a sticker.

94
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:09,440
Getting them in place takes simple tools.

95
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:16,000
The tools we use are just a measuring tape, a knife, a squeegee, and it's a boat.

96
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:21,260
Once the panels are installed, the detail work begins.

97
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:24,960
Like cutting holes for the brake lights and license plate.

98
00:07:24,961 --> 00:07:31,960
Then, the wrap is molded to the shape of the truck by heating the vinyl.

99
00:07:31,961 --> 00:07:39,960
You want to be really careful and you want to take your time because any wrong cut, we

100
00:07:39,961 --> 00:07:40,960
can show the paint.

101
00:07:40,961 --> 00:07:45,140
So a lot of patience when it comes to the detail.

102
00:07:45,141 --> 00:07:49,160
As our Menco's latest truck rolls out of the shop, it joins an ever-growing variety

103
00:07:49,161 --> 00:07:50,660
of food trucks.

104
00:07:50,661 --> 00:07:54,460
From Airstream trailers to school buses.

105
00:07:54,461 --> 00:07:57,840
Here's a peculiar one that travels on land and sea.

106
00:07:57,841 --> 00:07:59,700
An amphibious ice cream truck.

107
00:07:59,701 --> 00:08:04,640
Who's for ice cream?

108
00:08:04,641 --> 00:08:08,360
And then there's that double-decker pizza kitchen.

109
00:08:08,361 --> 00:08:16,500
A Scottish bus in its former life.

110
00:08:16,501 --> 00:08:21,740
What makes it doubly unusual is its chimney and its wood-fired oven that weighs 3,000

111
00:08:21,741 --> 00:08:24,280
pounds.

112
00:08:24,281 --> 00:08:29,280
No easy feat to accommodate, even on a food truck 30 feet long and 14 feet high.

113
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:38,080
We took out about 12,000 pounds of metal, of seats, and put in a whole bunch of stainless steel and mechanical equipment.

114
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:40,240
But first, the oven was built.

115
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:43,480
It's a brick-and-mortar oven on a stainless steel frame.

116
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:46,060
It sits right over the rear axles.

117
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:50,700
The chimney goes straight up through the top of the bus, kind of like a mini choo-choo train.

118
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:53,960
And that's how people know that we're there.

119
00:08:53,961 --> 00:08:58,460
they can smell the smoke, and we lure them in with that awesome wood-fired smell.

120
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:00,660
Margarita pizza?

121
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:02,320
Okay, hold your hand.

122
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:06,460
As more food trucks hit the streets,

123
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:09,880
many entrepreneurs feel they need a gimmick to draw customers in.

124
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:14,040
Some owners are going all out,

125
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:17,180
like this eye-catching food truck in downtown Seattle.

126
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:21,180
Meet Maximus Minimus, a giant mobile pig.

127
00:09:21,181 --> 00:09:23,780
The term we use is an urban assault pig.

128
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:28,320
It's kind of like what Mad Max would drive as a food truck.

129
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:29,900
The wrangler of the pig?

130
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:32,380
Restaurateur Kurt Beecher Dammeyer.

131
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:38,660
His inspiration for the food truck came from serving up a favorite dish to his restaurant staff,

132
00:09:39,060 --> 00:09:40,100
old pork sandwiches.

133
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:43,060
I love pork.

134
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:45,700
My desert island food would be pork.

135
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:50,200
Once we decided to do a truck selling pork, it had to be shaped like a pig.

136
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:53,340
But this isn't just any pig.

137
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:59,160
We wanted it to be rough, like maybe a Roman soldier would have used it because of the term

138
00:09:59,161 --> 00:10:04,093
maximus minimus. We wanted it to look like it might be an ancient warrior.

139
00:10:04,094 --> 00:10:05,160
I was really set

140
00:10:05,161 --> 00:10:12,200
on making this pig have a real kind of animus, if you will, and having it be something that wasn't

141
00:10:12,201 --> 00:10:12,901
silly.

142
00:10:12,902 --> 00:10:19,320
To create the mobile pig, Kurt bought a used food truck and then found the perfect

143
00:10:19,321 --> 00:10:25,380
candidate to transform the exterior. What we found is a rare combination of a guy

144
00:10:25,381 --> 00:10:31,260
who had been a kind of a boat builder but also an artist who'd actually worked

145
00:10:31,261 --> 00:10:36,660
on creating some pig-shaped objects. The outside of the truck was sheathed in a

146
00:10:36,661 --> 00:10:40,680
fiberglass skin that looks like metal and in three months this truck was

147
00:10:40,681 --> 00:10:46,860
turned into a cross between a Roman spaceship and a pig. In case there was

148
00:10:46,861 --> 00:10:54,940
any question where we got the sunglasses so food trucks are evolving into strange shapes

149
00:10:54,941 --> 00:11:01,180
and with the help of modern engineering into mind-boggling sizes too imagine a monster on

150
00:11:01,181 --> 00:11:09,820
wheels that transforms 12 000 pounds of food into a mega meal it's mega food trucks next on modern

151
00:11:09,821 --> 00:11:15,570
Marvels. We now return to Food Trucks on Modern Marvels.

152
00:11:15,571 --> 00:11:19,780
In America today, food trucks are all the

153
00:11:19,781 --> 00:11:24,157
rage, luring big crowds to these restaurants on wheels.

154
00:11:24,158 --> 00:11:27,420
But the ingenious designs of these trucks

155
00:11:27,421 --> 00:11:33,226
are finding another, more critical use as well.

156
00:11:33,227 --> 00:11:39,280
And on a mega scale, Saturday, August 27th, 2011,

157
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:47,820
Hurricane Irene, a powerful storm over 600 miles wide, makes landfall over eastern North

158
00:11:47,821 --> 00:11:52,500
Carolina.

159
00:11:52,501 --> 00:11:58,200
After hovering over the region for 24 hours, the storm finally turns its way north, leaving

160
00:11:58,201 --> 00:12:00,240
devastation in its wake.

161
00:12:00,241 --> 00:12:05,580
More than one million people without power in North Carolina alone.

162
00:12:05,581 --> 00:12:11,900
Fortunately, there's a solution, at least to the hunger problem.

163
00:12:11,901 --> 00:12:18,540
One of the biggest food trucks ever built rolls in to serve meals to the storm's victims.

164
00:12:18,541 --> 00:12:27,680
A mega food truck, 40 feet long and 8.5 feet wide, weighing in at a whopping 24,000 pounds.

165
00:12:27,681 --> 00:12:31,140
This monster is a transformer on wheels.

166
00:12:31,141 --> 00:12:36,300
side on poles to form deck areas, creating a kitchen space almost a thousand square feet

167
00:12:36,301 --> 00:12:37,300
in size.

168
00:12:37,301 --> 00:12:44,220
The back of the truck also folds out and transforms into a hydration deck with a three compartment

169
00:12:44,221 --> 00:12:45,220
sink.

170
00:12:45,221 --> 00:12:53,260
At full capacity, the truck can support 60 cooks and serve up to 30,000 meals a day.

171
00:12:53,261 --> 00:12:57,800
With the help of support trucks, the mega truck can feed for weeks on end.

172
00:12:57,801 --> 00:13:03,900
In preparation for today's crowds, supply trucks hold up to 70,000 pounds of food each,

173
00:13:03,901 --> 00:13:07,800
so heavy that one of them actually punched a hole in the pavement.

174
00:13:07,801 --> 00:13:14,240
As long as the food count is up, we'll be getting one of these about every other day.

175
00:13:14,241 --> 00:13:17,580
Today's meal count, at least 8,000 hungry people.

176
00:13:17,581 --> 00:13:21,040
That's almost 12,000 pounds of food.

177
00:13:21,041 --> 00:13:26,420
To carry out this gigantic meal, 30 volunteer cooks from a local Baptist group team up with

178
00:13:26,421 --> 00:13:28,420
the American Red Cross.

179
00:13:28,421 --> 00:13:30,420
The Red Cross brings all the food in.

180
00:13:30,421 --> 00:13:34,420
We do the cooking and they do the serving of most of it.

181
00:13:34,421 --> 00:13:40,420
We put a heavy liner in the Cambros and they feed right out of these Cambros.

182
00:13:40,421 --> 00:13:42,420
Is your power bent out?

183
00:13:42,421 --> 00:13:44,420
That's wonderful.

184
00:13:44,421 --> 00:13:46,420
Look this way and give you a big smile.

185
00:13:46,421 --> 00:13:50,420
At the heart of the Chaper is the truck.

186
00:13:50,421 --> 00:13:52,420
Full of custom equipment.

187
00:13:52,421 --> 00:13:54,420
Lots of it.

188
00:13:54,421 --> 00:13:56,360
like two roll-in rack ovens

189
00:13:56,361 --> 00:14:00,200
and four convection ovens for meat.

190
00:14:01,420 --> 00:14:05,560
Total rack count, 54 racks at 24 patties a rack.

191
00:14:05,880 --> 00:14:09,560
That's 1,296 meals that can be cooked at one time.

192
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:16,360
Then, there are the two 40-gallon and two 30-gallon capacity tilt skillets

193
00:14:16,361 --> 00:14:19,680
to cook items like beans, ravioli, grits, and eggs.

194
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:22,460
The reason it's called a tilt skillet,

195
00:14:22,461 --> 00:14:24,480
it tilts over and has a spout on it,

196
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:26,480
you can pour those right into the can for it.

197
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:31,720
It's basically like a giant version of your skillet at home.

198
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:35,440
To open so many gallons of food,

199
00:14:35,860 --> 00:14:38,160
the cooks rely on two pneumatic can openers.

200
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:42,300
Using suction, each can open up to 12 cans a minute.

201
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:46,760
Still, there are six to eight people doing nothing but opening cans.

202
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:51,600
No deep fryers on here.

203
00:14:52,460 --> 00:14:58,220
healthy food to power up all those ovens and skillets the truck is loaded with

204
00:14:58,221 --> 00:15:04,520
four 200 gallon tanks of propane enough to last through two days of cooking

205
00:15:04,521 --> 00:15:09,500
and the whole operation is powered by a mega sized generator 45 kilowatts strong

206
00:15:09,501 --> 00:15:16,640
that's enough energy to power up a small town this kitchen is self-sufficient we

207
00:15:16,641 --> 00:15:21,560
can pull this kitchen in if we have no water no electricity or anything we can

208
00:15:21,561 --> 00:15:24,160
set it up and run.

209
00:15:24,161 --> 00:15:30,320
Today's mega food trucks can hold tremendous amounts of weight, up to 54,000 pounds, and

210
00:15:30,321 --> 00:15:36,560
are designed to withstand years of use.

211
00:15:36,561 --> 00:15:41,520
AA Cater Truck in Los Angeles, California pioneered the technology that makes mega food

212
00:15:41,521 --> 00:15:44,080
trucks possible.

213
00:15:44,081 --> 00:15:51,720
At peak times, AA employs more than 150 workers who mainly build food trucks from scratch.

214
00:15:51,721 --> 00:15:57,440
It takes 30 days for the team to assemble each truck, using an ingenious concept developed

215
00:15:57,441 --> 00:16:04,040
by the company's founder, Vahe Karapetian.

216
00:16:04,041 --> 00:16:10,000
In the 1970s, the early days of food truck manufacturing, Vahe saw that many food trucks

217
00:16:10,001 --> 00:16:14,000
were not built to withstand the rigors of kitchen use.

218
00:16:14,001 --> 00:16:18,760
He saw how steam and water from daily cooking could cause the steel bodies to rust from

219
00:16:18,761 --> 00:16:21,180
the inside out.

220
00:16:21,181 --> 00:16:27,720
And how thousands of pounds of kitchen equipment could eventually make trucks sag on the sides.

221
00:16:27,721 --> 00:16:31,380
In 1985, he came up with a solution.

222
00:16:31,381 --> 00:16:33,820
What's called a uniframe truck body.

223
00:16:33,821 --> 00:16:38,800
A revolutionary new approach to designing trucks, enabling them to handle more weight

224
00:16:38,801 --> 00:16:42,120
while resisting corrosion.

225
00:16:42,121 --> 00:16:46,320
Vahe introduced aircraft-grade aluminum to replace the old steel truck bodies.

226
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:49,200
Aluminum is more corrosion resistant than steel.

227
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:56,020
For strength, he added an interlocking beam design to accommodate the heavy loads.

228
00:16:57,240 --> 00:17:00,320
To make the truck's floor, which undergoes enormous stress,

229
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:05,140
his team interlocks two-inch thick aluminum beams together to form a platform.

230
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:11,380
Then they add L-shaped brackets called extrusions to the floor beams.

231
00:17:11,381 --> 00:17:13,040
for another layer of support.

232
00:17:14,540 --> 00:17:16,860
The sidewalls are strengthened by vertical beams,

233
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:18,880
then skinned with sheet metal.

234
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:24,100
Finally, the floor is interlocked with the sidewalls.

235
00:17:25,280 --> 00:17:28,020
Together, all of this creates the uniframe body,

236
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:31,360
strong enough to withstand the rigors of constant movement.

237
00:17:33,300 --> 00:17:36,260
Today, engineers have adapted some of the same principles,

238
00:17:36,261 --> 00:17:38,000
like framing and interlocking,

239
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:40,620
to the job of retrofitting food trucks, too.

240
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:47,060
The Uniframe Strength body is so robust, it's even used in SWAT vehicles.

241
00:17:49,340 --> 00:17:52,720
In 2005, during Hurricanes Katrina, Rita, and Wilma,

242
00:17:53,240 --> 00:17:56,980
thousands of disaster relief food trucks were mobilized for over three months,

243
00:17:57,540 --> 00:17:59,800
serving more than 68 million meals.

244
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:02,620
Food trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

245
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:08,100
We now return to food trucks on Modern Marvels.

246
00:18:08,101 --> 00:18:15,100
Today, nearly 3 million food trucks troll the streets of American cities and towns, with

247
00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:18,690
at least 10 new ones starting up each week.

248
00:18:18,691 --> 00:18:21,600
But it might surprise you that the roots of

249
00:18:21,601 --> 00:18:24,702
this trend go back more than a century.

250
00:18:24,703 --> 00:18:28,600
The very edge of rural America, Indian Territory.

251
00:18:33,180 --> 00:18:37,180
Food trucks rose out of the need to feed working men on the road.

252
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:43,100
And in the 1800s, some of those men were the cattlemen of the American West.

253
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:51,440
In 1866, Texas rancher Charles Goodnight was preparing for a huge cattle drive,

254
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:56,820
moving a herd of 2,000 longhorns from Texas to an army post in what's now New Mexico.

255
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:03,700
The journey would take eight weeks on a dangerous trail through Indian territory.

256
00:19:03,701 --> 00:19:11,820
Much of it was desert, where food would be hard to come by.

257
00:19:11,821 --> 00:19:17,320
So Goodnight modified a durable army surplus wagon called a Studebaker and invented the

258
00:19:17,321 --> 00:19:21,160
Chuck Wagon, a kitchen on wheels.

259
00:19:21,161 --> 00:19:25,800
He mounted a wooden box to the back and added shelves and compartments to store condiments

260
00:19:25,801 --> 00:19:29,020
and cooking utensils.

261
00:19:29,021 --> 00:19:34,220
A hinged lid opened up to become the cook's workstation, as well as a table to serve food.

262
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:42,120
Food on the cattle drives was simple and easy to keep, like beans and salted meats, biscuits and coffee.

263
00:19:43,960 --> 00:19:50,200
To the cowboys, Chuck was slang for good, heartwarming food, so the wagon became known as the Chuck Wagon.

264
00:19:50,201 --> 00:19:56,200
On the other side of the country, in the cities of New England,

265
00:19:56,620 --> 00:19:59,100
another kind of kitchen-on-wheels was taking off.

266
00:20:00,140 --> 00:20:03,360
The lunch wagon, whose night version was known as the night owl.

267
00:20:05,100 --> 00:20:07,460
Force-drawn streetcars were joining their ranks, too.

268
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:13,200
As these wagons became obsolete, they were often retrofitted into food venues.

269
00:20:14,900 --> 00:20:18,640
When they lost their wheels, they gave birth to the American Diner.

270
00:20:20,200 --> 00:20:25,000
All of these early food trucks lured customers with their convenience.

271
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:30,340
But there was another kind of food truck that took the experience of mobile dining to a whole new level.

272
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:39,680
Romancing customers and forcing the kitchen on wheels to keep pace with an internal combustion engine.

273
00:20:41,640 --> 00:20:44,200
The heyday of the dining car has passed.

274
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:49,440
But this very civilized custom is still a big draw.

275
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:52,300
on historic trains throughout America.

276
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:54,460
In New Hampshire,

277
00:20:55,800 --> 00:20:58,540
the Conway Scenic Railroad runs through the White Mountains.

278
00:21:01,240 --> 00:21:03,320
We are in the dining cart of Gorwa.

279
00:21:03,640 --> 00:21:06,040
It's been refurbished in the interior

280
00:21:06,041 --> 00:21:10,840
to take it back in time to what it might have looked like in 1929.

281
00:21:15,760 --> 00:21:17,740
Like today's gourmet food trucks,

282
00:21:17,741 --> 00:21:20,680
This mobile eatery has a commercial kitchen on board,

283
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:24,700
is capable of creating a four-course meal as the old cross-country trains.

284
00:21:25,880 --> 00:21:30,160
All of the food is prepared on board, and fresh food is brought on the train

285
00:21:30,161 --> 00:21:33,440
to accommodate the folks that we have orders for that day.

286
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:38,360
Today, Chef Eli Charette and his team of three cooks

287
00:21:38,361 --> 00:21:41,540
need to whip up a three-course lunch for about 40 passengers.

288
00:21:43,260 --> 00:21:45,360
They do this up to three times a day,

289
00:21:45,361 --> 00:21:48,940
mostly in a kitchen only 78 square feet in size.

290
00:21:50,220 --> 00:21:53,520
And the team shares a challenge with earlier trained chefs, too.

291
00:21:54,260 --> 00:21:55,800
Their kitchen is always moving.

292
00:21:56,740 --> 00:21:58,960
We bounce around a lot. We bump into things.

293
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:02,900
When we're traveling back foot between the two kitchen cars here,

294
00:22:03,160 --> 00:22:05,520
we get thrashed around.

295
00:22:05,740 --> 00:22:08,960
Occasionally, things fall over, and it's mayhem.

296
00:22:09,420 --> 00:22:12,720
We typically go about 20 miles an hour, which isn't too fast.

297
00:22:12,721 --> 00:22:19,720
We try to maintain our track so that it isn't too rough.

298
00:22:19,721 --> 00:22:22,720
For the servers, too, it's a balancing act.

299
00:22:22,721 --> 00:22:29,720
Try and hold both of them as steady as possible,

300
00:22:29,721 --> 00:22:33,720
and if one moves, you better hope the other hand follows.

301
00:22:33,721 --> 00:22:37,720
The dining car may be the most glamorous food truck predecessor,

302
00:22:37,721 --> 00:22:41,720
but there's another one that may inspire even more affection.

303
00:22:41,721 --> 00:22:44,280
The good old-fashioned hot dog cart.

304
00:22:45,640 --> 00:22:48,640
We now return to Food Trucks on Modern Marvel.

305
00:22:50,160 --> 00:22:53,300
Hot dog carts first appeared in the late 19th century.

306
00:22:54,300 --> 00:22:59,320
Called dog wagons, they became popular with hungry visitors at places like baseball games,

307
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:02,740
the Chicago World's Fair, and New York's Coney Island.

308
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:07,520
The cart's two-by-three-foot size gave it the mobility to fit into small spaces

309
00:23:07,521 --> 00:23:10,760
and weave through narrow streets and sidewalks.

310
00:23:11,720 --> 00:23:17,800
Today, no matter what city you live in, you can be sure to find a hot dog cart.

311
00:23:18,340 --> 00:23:19,240
Can I have a hot dog?

312
00:23:19,400 --> 00:23:20,380
Yes, I can do that.

313
00:23:20,640 --> 00:23:26,188
Rocky Segura sells the American classic, hot dogs boiled in water, a.k.a.

314
00:23:26,189 --> 00:23:27,480
dirty water dogs.

315
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:31,380
It's something that came out of New York because it's sitting in water.

316
00:23:31,940 --> 00:23:34,840
It's just a myth. The water's not dirty. The hot dog is good.

317
00:23:35,380 --> 00:23:38,360
It's just a way of preparing the hot dogs in New York.

318
00:23:38,920 --> 00:23:39,660
Can I get a hot dog, please?

319
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:40,820
Yes, sir. Two dogs.

320
00:23:40,821 --> 00:23:45,160
To cook a New York-style hot dog, you need a New York-style hot dog cart.

321
00:23:45,640 --> 00:23:50,500
You see them all around the world, but the largest manufacturer of this classic food truck is in Miami.

322
00:23:52,920 --> 00:23:58,300
Louis de Raimondo, the self-proclaimed hot dog king, and his team at the All-American Hot Dog Company,

323
00:23:58,940 --> 00:24:02,420
manufacture up to 1,200 New York-style street carts a year.

324
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:06,720
It has three pans to boil or steam the hot dogs.

325
00:24:06,721 --> 00:24:09,000
It has two burners, two control knobs.

326
00:24:09,001 --> 00:24:11,000
The hot dogs are in here.

327
00:24:11,001 --> 00:24:14,000
Each pan can hold up to 50 hot dogs,

328
00:24:14,001 --> 00:24:16,000
but a vendor doesn't cook them all at once.

329
00:24:16,001 --> 00:24:18,000
He's cooking as he's selling.

330
00:24:18,001 --> 00:24:19,000
One hot dog.

331
00:24:19,001 --> 00:24:21,000
Thank you so much.

332
00:24:21,001 --> 00:24:24,000
A vendor can cook about 200 hot dogs an hour

333
00:24:24,001 --> 00:24:26,000
and in a popular location,

334
00:24:26,001 --> 00:24:29,000
make over $100,000 a year.

335
00:24:29,001 --> 00:24:32,000
The success of this classic food truck

336
00:24:32,001 --> 00:24:34,000
may well be due to its simple design.

337
00:24:34,001 --> 00:24:38,000
It uses a rudimentary cooking method,

338
00:24:38,001 --> 00:24:39,200
And it's easy to maneuver.

339
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:42,100
To make the hot dog cart,

340
00:24:42,440 --> 00:24:44,560
Louis and his team start with an aluminum frame

341
00:24:44,561 --> 00:24:47,560
and install a basic burner system for boiling water.

342
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:51,580
They use quarter-inch copper gas lines

343
00:24:51,581 --> 00:24:54,920
to connect two cast-iron burners to a 20-pound propane tank.

344
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:00,900
And since the vendor will be handling food,

345
00:25:01,360 --> 00:25:03,900
Louis and his team add a water system with a hand sink.

346
00:25:05,740 --> 00:25:07,360
It relies on gravity.

347
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:11,720
Water flows from the 5-gallon water tank above into the sink below.

348
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:16,680
For mobility, this pushable cart has a 10-inch front dolly wheel,

349
00:25:17,180 --> 00:25:20,560
which makes it easy for a man, woman, or even a child to push it around.

350
00:25:21,960 --> 00:25:26,300
But loaded up, a typical mobile pushcart weighs 400 pounds.

351
00:25:27,300 --> 00:25:30,000
Little has changed in this earliest food truck's design

352
00:25:30,001 --> 00:25:33,700
since the first sausages were sold from pushcarts in the 19th century.

353
00:25:33,701 --> 00:25:37,840
But back then, the carts were made out of wood and fueled by kerosene.

354
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:42,160
Today, you can boil the dogs, steam them, or grill them.

355
00:25:42,540 --> 00:25:45,820
Just as long as you don't break the cardinal rule of hot dogs.

356
00:25:46,160 --> 00:25:48,380
You cannot put ketchup on a hot dog.

357
00:25:49,940 --> 00:25:52,400
You cannot. It's a rule.

358
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:55,720
Because it belongs on a hamburger, not on a hot dog.

359
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:59,880
Basic hot dog is mustard, not ketchup.

360
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:02,200
Just mustard.

361
00:26:02,740 --> 00:26:07,200
Hot dog carts inspire memories of childhood baseball games and summer barbecues.

362
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:13,320
And of another nostalgic food truck that many of us grew up with, the ice cream truck.

363
00:26:14,380 --> 00:26:16,865
The first good humor trucks, a.k.a.

364
00:26:16,866 --> 00:26:21,480
sales cars, were converted automobiles and bicycles in the 1920s.

365
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:28,180
They carried around prepackaged treats, using blocks of dry ice to keep all those fudgesicles frozen.

366
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:35,120
But today, some ice cream trucks are upping the ante.

367
00:26:35,121 --> 00:26:37,500
Take Los Angeles' Lake Street Creamery,

368
00:26:37,780 --> 00:26:40,520
the shop and kitchen of Tim Ferguson and Beth Cola.

369
00:26:41,480 --> 00:26:43,480
This isn't your mother's ice cream truck.

370
00:26:44,120 --> 00:26:46,540
In fact, there's no free packaging allowed.

371
00:26:46,980 --> 00:26:48,380
We make all the ice cream on the truck.

372
00:26:48,500 --> 00:26:49,400
Everything is right here.

373
00:26:49,640 --> 00:26:51,720
This is our big kitchen.

374
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:53,420
This is our ice cream maker.

375
00:26:53,560 --> 00:26:56,600
It takes about 8 to 10 minutes to do a batch of ice cream.

376
00:26:58,520 --> 00:26:59,920
For their custom truck,

377
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:02,800
Beth and Tim retrofitted a used step van

378
00:27:02,801 --> 00:27:04,800
around a few key pieces of equipment.

379
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:07,700
a dipping cabinet to store the vats of ice cream,

380
00:27:07,701 --> 00:27:12,260
and a batch freezer, also known as an ice cream maker.

381
00:27:13,260 --> 00:27:17,060
Typically a gallon of mix of ingredients yields about six quarts.

382
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:21,120
That was the biggest batch freezer that would fit on the truck.

383
00:27:21,800 --> 00:27:26,280
For even weight distribution, the equipment is placed diagonally on each side.

384
00:27:26,680 --> 00:27:29,520
Water and waste tanks are also placed to offset the weight.

385
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:33,180
The dipping cabinet alone weighs 600 pounds,

386
00:27:33,181 --> 00:27:36,220
filled with ice cream, add another 600.

387
00:27:37,160 --> 00:27:39,900
To power up the gas-guzzling 240-bowl

388
00:27:39,901 --> 00:27:42,800
ice cream machine, there's a 10-kilowatt generator.

389
00:27:45,560 --> 00:27:48,240
And to lure customers, the Duo cut out

390
00:27:48,241 --> 00:27:49,940
a larger serving window than most

391
00:27:49,941 --> 00:27:51,900
so people could see them inside.

392
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:56,540
On a good day, Tim and Beth scooped 15 tubs,

393
00:27:56,960 --> 00:27:58,580
or about 800 scoops.

394
00:27:59,540 --> 00:28:01,280
My arm was like a noodle.

395
00:28:01,281 --> 00:28:03,800
Yeah, until I learned how to scoop properly,

396
00:28:03,980 --> 00:28:06,800
like, this arm was literally bigger than the other arm.

397
00:28:06,880 --> 00:28:07,580
You had a Popeye arm.

398
00:28:07,581 --> 00:28:08,680
I had a Popeye arm.

399
00:28:08,820 --> 00:28:11,480
It was just, it may still be a little bigger than the other one.

400
00:28:13,400 --> 00:28:16,200
Popeye arms, demanding customers.

401
00:28:16,640 --> 00:28:17,340
I'm ready.

402
00:28:17,780 --> 00:28:19,440
Kitchen's the size of a pool table.

403
00:28:20,540 --> 00:28:22,800
Coming up, from dawn to dusk,

404
00:28:23,260 --> 00:28:25,260
a day in the life of a food truck.

405
00:28:25,261 --> 00:28:34,940
Mobile canteens were used during wartime to boost the morale of field troops.

406
00:28:34,941 --> 00:28:41,040
The most common mobile field kitchen was developed during the Korean War, called the MKT or Mobile

407
00:28:41,041 --> 00:28:42,040
Kitchen Trailer.

408
00:28:42,041 --> 00:28:45,060
It can be set up in just half an hour.

409
00:28:45,061 --> 00:28:47,940
Food trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

410
00:28:47,941 --> 00:28:55,020
We now return to food trucks on Modern Marvels.

411
00:28:55,021 --> 00:28:58,780
Today's gourmet food trucks are getting a lot of attention,

412
00:28:58,781 --> 00:29:04,500
luring thousands of office workers out to the streets and cities across America.

413
00:29:04,501 --> 00:29:10,700
But there's another kind of food truck that's been filling a huge gap since the early 1970s.

414
00:29:10,701 --> 00:29:15,460
A breed of sturdy trucks that serves the blue-collar workforce.

415
00:29:15,461 --> 00:29:18,740
The ubiquitous Roach Coach.

416
00:29:18,741 --> 00:29:23,060
The name comes from an old myth that the trucks weren't sanitary.

417
00:29:23,061 --> 00:29:28,060
You got that old saying, oh, you don't eat off the food truck, so you might be eating a dog or something like that.

418
00:29:28,440 --> 00:29:29,940
But that's just an old wives' tale.

419
00:29:30,460 --> 00:29:33,080
In truth, they're regulated by local health departments.

420
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:39,320
Today, they're known for serving hot food at good prices.

421
00:29:40,060 --> 00:29:41,260
Take a inside burrito.

422
00:29:42,180 --> 00:29:45,400
On construction sites and in factory parking lots.

423
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,540
Alfredo Reyes drives a Roach Coach in Los Angeles.

424
00:29:50,020 --> 00:29:53,040
One of thousands that crisscross this city every day.

425
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:56,840
4 a.m.

426
00:29:58,360 --> 00:30:02,540
Alfredo's daily routine begins at a huge parking lot in downtown Los Angeles.

427
00:30:04,420 --> 00:30:06,360
It's one of 20 in L.A. alone.

428
00:30:06,940 --> 00:30:10,200
Home base to 120 trucks that run around the clock.

429
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:14,260
Alfredo and the other drivers get the trucks ready to head out,

430
00:30:14,900 --> 00:30:18,760
loading 40 gallons of water and 30 gallons of propane on each truck.

431
00:30:19,960 --> 00:30:22,120
Ice comes from a 30-ton ice maker.

432
00:30:23,060 --> 00:30:28,060
Alfredo and his cook Graciela stock up on food at the Lotz grocery store.

433
00:30:28,061 --> 00:30:34,060
She's gonna make some special like tacos, burritos, quesadillas.

434
00:30:34,061 --> 00:30:37,060
They need to buy enough to last the whole day.

435
00:30:37,061 --> 00:30:40,060
If they run out, they'll lose customers.

436
00:30:40,061 --> 00:30:43,060
You lose profit and that never can happen.

437
00:30:43,061 --> 00:30:47,060
Eight dozen eggs, 22 pounds of meat,

438
00:30:47,061 --> 00:30:52,060
fresh produce, and a couple hundred tortillas.

439
00:30:52,061 --> 00:30:59,060
We do try to serve today maybe 220 to 30 so we're gonna buy for 20 extra people just

440
00:30:59,061 --> 00:31:02,060
in case we have some extra guys we don't wanna run out.

441
00:31:02,061 --> 00:31:06,060
One house pound, one cook, seven tomatoes.

442
00:31:08,060 --> 00:31:13,060
After years of working this beat, the duo has a routine in place.

443
00:31:13,061 --> 00:31:19,060
We already know what the customers want because they're usually a regular customer.

444
00:31:19,061 --> 00:31:22,240
So before we get to the stop, sure you have the items ready.

445
00:31:29,940 --> 00:31:31,340
5.30 a.m.

446
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:33,820
Alfredo and Graciela hit the road.

447
00:31:34,960 --> 00:31:37,360
On their route, nine stops.

448
00:31:37,820 --> 00:31:40,020
They have to do it twice in just seven hours.

449
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:42,380
One stop every 15 minutes.

450
00:31:45,920 --> 00:31:46,980
6 a.m.

451
00:31:46,981 --> 00:31:49,580
Alfredo arrives at the first stop on schedule.

452
00:31:51,020 --> 00:31:52,960
Good thing. His customers are waiting.

453
00:31:53,900 --> 00:31:54,880
We have the small ones.

454
00:31:55,440 --> 00:31:56,880
I only have the large ones, baby.

455
00:31:57,740 --> 00:31:59,080
We definitely look forward to the food truck.

456
00:31:59,540 --> 00:32:00,780
I get a chicken burrito every day.

457
00:32:01,100 --> 00:32:02,040
I love the chicken burrito.

458
00:32:02,860 --> 00:32:03,800
Got to wash my waist.

459
00:32:07,980 --> 00:32:09,900
Uno de pescado, uno de chorizo.

460
00:32:10,400 --> 00:32:12,360
That's our only break for the day, so...

461
00:32:12,361 --> 00:32:15,480
There you go.

462
00:32:15,481 --> 00:32:20,480
We have 30 minute breaks, so you gotta hurry up and eat and pretty much get back to work.

463
00:32:20,481 --> 00:32:30,480
The restaurant, you gotta wait 10, 15, 20 minutes for them to serve you.

464
00:32:30,481 --> 00:32:34,480
In the truck, you only, the maximum you gotta wait 2 to 3 minutes.

465
00:32:34,481 --> 00:32:36,480
It looks really good. Tastes good as well.

466
00:32:36,481 --> 00:32:40,480
When they come and tell you, you know what, this is a good food, that will make you feel good.

467
00:32:40,481 --> 00:32:43,480
Because that means you're doing a good job.

468
00:32:45,480 --> 00:32:50,480
The Roach Coach provides a service many foremen have come to depend on.

469
00:32:50,481 --> 00:32:56,480
I've known him for a few years, and he comes with me on every job I come on because he's a good truck.

470
00:32:56,481 --> 00:32:59,480
It's a toss-up when you get one of these wagons.

471
00:32:59,481 --> 00:33:06,480
We call them Roach Coaches out in the field because, you know, it's meat when you order, and sometimes it's mystery meat.

472
00:33:06,481 --> 00:33:12,480
So when you find a good lunch truck like he is, you keep that lunch truck, and you keep them rolling every job.

473
00:33:12,481 --> 00:33:23,040
every job when we get a job he's my guy he's a good one it's always nice to see

